curl -fsSL "http://localhost:9090/federate?match%5B%5D=%7B__name__%3D~%22up%22%7D" \
  | grep -E "^up"
# Expected: lines containing cluster="cluster-a" and region="us-east-1"

# Verify cluster-b federation endpoint
curl -fsSL "http://localhost:9091/federate?match%5B%5D=%7B__name__%3D~%22up%22%7D" \
  | grep -E "^up"
# Expected: lines containing cluster="cluster-b" and region="us-west-2"

# Query the global instance for up â both clusters must appear
curl -fsSL "http://localhost:9092/api/v1/query?query=up" \
  | python3 -c "
import sys, json
payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
for r in payload['data']['result']:
    print(r['metric'].get('cluster','MISSING'), r['metric'].get('instance'), r['value'][1])
"
Confirmation: The Python snippet prints at least one row containing cluster-a and at least one row containing cluster-b, with value 1 for each healthy target.

Step 2.2 â Author cross-cluster recording rules and a federation health alert
Recording rules (pre-computed expressions stored as new time series) reduce query latency and allow the global instance to expose stable metric names that downstream dashboards or alerting systems can rely on regardless of which cluster the data originated from. Write a rules.yml file at /etc/prometheus/federation/rules.yml that satisfies the following requirements:

A group named cluster_aggregates containing three recording rules:

cluster:node_cpu_utilisation:avg1m â per-cluster average CPU utilisation (non-idle fraction) over a 1-minute window, preserving the cluster label
cluster:node_memory_available_bytes:avg â per-cluster average available memory in bytes, preserving the cluster label
global:node_cpu_utilisation:avg1m â single scalar representing the mean CPU utilisation across all clusters (no cluster label on the output)
A group named federation_health containing one alerting rule:

Alert name FederationTargetDown that fires when any target scraped by a job whose name begins with federate- has up == 0 for more than two minutes, with a severity="critical" label and an annotation summary that includes the value of {{ $labels.instance }}.
After writing the file, reload the federation instance without restarting it (use the /-/reload lifecycle endpoint enabled by --web.enable-lifecycle), then confirm the rules appear in the API response.

sudo tee /etc/prometheus/federation/rules.yml > /dev/null <<'EOF'
groups:
  - name: cluster_aggregates
    interval: 30s
    rules:
      - record: "cluster:node_cpu_utilisation:avg1m"
        expr: |
          1 - avg by (cluster) (
            rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[1m])
          )

      - record: "cluster:node_memory_available_bytes:avg"
        expr: |
          avg by (cluster) (node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes)

      - record: "global:node_cpu_utilisation:avg1m"
        expr: |
          avg(cluster:node_cpu_utilisation:avg1m)

  - name: federation_health
    rules:
      - alert: FederationTargetDown
        expr: up{job=~"federate-.*"} == 0
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: "critical"
        annotations:
          summary: "Federation target {{ $labels.instance }} is unreachable"
          description: "Job {{ $labels.job }} has been unable to scrape {{ $labels.instance }} for more than 2 minutes."
EOF

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/federation/rules.yml

# Validate the rules file before reloading
promtool check rules /etc/prometheus/federation/rules.yml

# Reload the federation instance via the lifecycle API (no restart required)
curl -fsSL -X POST http://localhost:9092/-/reload
# Official lifecycle API docs: https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/management_api/

# Wait for the evaluation cycle, then confirm rules are loaded
sleep 35
curl -fsSL "http://localhost:9092/api/v1/rules" \
  | python3 -c "
import sys, json
payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
for g in payload['data']['groups']:
