Confirmation: The Python output lists both groups (cluster_aggregates, federation_health) and all four rules by name. promtool check rules exits with status 0 and prints SUCCESS.

Task 3: Implement a Synthetic Workload Exporter and Validate End-to-End Aggregation
Step 3.1 â Deploy a custom metrics exporter that simulates two-cluster application traffic
A production federation topology is only meaningful when cluster-level instances carry application-specific metrics in addition to infrastructure metrics. Implement a Python-based HTTP metrics exporter conforming to the interface contract below. Deploy two instances of it â one representing Cluster A traffic (port 8080) and one representing Cluster B traffic (port 8081) â as systemd services owned by the prometheus user.

Each instance must expose the following metrics in the Prometheus text exposition format at the /metrics path:

app_http_requests_total â counter, labels: method, status_code, endpoint
app_request_duration_seconds â histogram with buckets [0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5], labels: endpoint
app_active_sessions â gauge, no labels
The exporter must generate realistic variance: request counts must increment on every scrape, latency observations must vary across the histogram buckets, and active sessions must fluctuate within a plausible range (50â500). The exporter must not require any external Python packages beyond prometheus_client.

